  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 214 PM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe axis of a deep mid-level trough and an associated sharp cold \nfront will be quickly advancing through Georgia as the long term \nperiod begins, which will clear the forecast area to the southeast \nby mid-morning on Saturday. This cold front will be largely dry, \nwith rain chances of 10% or less in portions of far north and east \nGeorgia. There will be a strong pressure gradient in place behind \nthe front and ahead of a surface high (1035+ mb) approaching from \nthe Mid Mississippi River Valley. The pressure gradient will \ncontribute to breezy northwesterly winds between 15-20 mph and gusts \nup to 30 mph through much of the day on Saturday. A significantly \ncooler airmass will also set up over the region in the wake of the \nfront. Low temperatures on Saturday morning are expected to be in \nthe mid to upper 40s in areas north of I-85 and in the low to mid \n50s to the south. As cooler air becomes more established, afternoon \nhighs will range from near 60 in the far northern tier to the low \n70s in south-central Georgia. \n\nBroad mid-level ridging will become established over the east CONUS \nSaturday night into Sunday as the aforementioned surface high moves \neast across the Appalachians. Strong radiational cooling under clear \nskies will lead to the coolest temperatures of the period early \nSunday morning, with widespread 40s and even some mid to upper 30s \nin the higher elevations of far northeast Georgia. As the high \ncontinues east of the Appalachians, a CAD wedge is anticipated to \nspread into north Georgia, keeping highs limited to the low 60s in \nportions of northeast Georgia compared to mid to upper 60s elsewhere \nacross the region. Breezy easterly winds will also be present within \nthis wedge. A gradual warming trend will begin to start next week as \nthe surface high moves into the west Atlantic and the aforementioned \nridge becomes centered over the Southeast and Mid-Atlantic coast. \nThis high pressure regime will continue to keep conditions dry into \nnext week.",
